UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037010840?origin=resultslist
AD  - Maulana Azad National Institute of Technology, Department of Humanities and Social Sciences, Bhopal, India
AB  - The growing presence of generative AI-based tools in higher education has brought up urgent rhetoric questions as to the impact on students (higher-order thinking) and especially critical thinking. The authors investigate the dependence between attitude to AI use and the critical thinking skills of Indian engineering students. The data was obtained by means of a structured survey among undergraduate engineering students, which included the validated Critical Thinking Questionnaire (CThQ) and questions addressing the trends in the use of generative AI. High-frequency and low-frequency AI users were identified based on self-reports of frequency. There was the independent-samples t-test, which was done to compare the critical thinking scores of these groups. The results show that in spite of the fact that low-frequency AI users achieved marginally higher mean critical thinking scores with a high-frequency user, the difference was not significant in statistical terms. These findings indicate that the frequency of AI usage is insignificant in forecasting critical thinking skill. Rather, the results highlight the need to investigate qualitative aspects of AI use, including patterns of dependence and cognitive offloading, which, rather than usage frequency, determine usage frequency. The work helps in providing context-dependent empirical data in the Indian engineering education and makes suggestions to AI literacy programs and the responsible introduction of AI tools within the educational process.  © 2026 IEEE.
