AB  - The recent advancement of artificial intelligence (AI) technology has revolutionized the language translation industry and unavoidably exerts an impact on student translators in multiple ways. In this context, this study investigated the antecedents of student translators’ self-efficacy in AI-enhanced contexts, an underexplored area in current academic research. Data were collected via validated quantitative scales from 446 undergraduate student translators across eight public universities in China, who self-reported their AI literacy, technology acceptance, self-regulation, and self-efficacy in leveraging AI tools for translation tasks. Results indicate that AI literacy, technology acceptance, and self-regulation positively predicted translation self-efficacy. Furthermore, AI literacy served as a positive predictor of technology acceptance and self-regulation in AI-assisted translation, while technology acceptance positively predicted self-regulation. Additionally, technology acceptance and self-regulated learning (SRL) acted as partial mediators between AI literacy and translation self-efficacy, and the serial mediation pathway ‘‘AI literacy → technology acceptance → SRL → translation self-efficacy’’ was confirmed. This research offers theoretical and practical insights for translation education research and pedagogy. © 2013 IEEE.
