AB  - The aim of this study is to investigate how systems thinking can support the technical teaching of electronics and to propose a framework to identify educational strategies that promote this approach. A hybrid methodology was adopted, integrating both quantitative and qualitative approaches. The qualitative approach involved a Systematic Literature Review (SLR) to outline the research field and identify knowledge gaps. The quantitative approach included a bibliometric analysis to confirm the relevance of the research. The PAPER method was used to integrate the SLR and bibliometric analysis and organize systems thinking elements into four categories: characteristics of systems thinking; teaching methodologies; modeling and analysis tools; and systems thinking competencies. The results indicate that systems thinking enhances knowledge, learning, and holistic understanding in electronics education. As an original contribution, a framework was proposed to integrate educational strategies for developing systems thinking in technical electronics education. As future work, empirical validation of the framework through questionnaires and practical experiments with stakeholders in electronics education is suggested. It is concluded that system thinking represents an appropriate and efficient methodology or tool for application in electronics teaching.  © 2025 The Authors.
