AB  - To address persistent challenges in Electrical Engineering education for non-electrical majors-including the gap between theoretical instruction and practical application, insufficient development of engineering thinking, and limited hands-on experience with complex systems-this paper proposes an integrated, multi-level, and student competency-oriented teaching model. The model adopts the dSPACE real-time simulation platform as the core environment for engineering practice, tightly integrates project-based learning, designs projects aligned with industry-education-research collaboration, and incorporates a large language model-based AI agent to provide personalized learning support. Teaching practice demonstrates that the proposed model establishes a closed-loop engineering training framework encompassing theoretical learning, simulation modeling, and hardware-in-the-loop validation. Experimental and assessment results indicate significant improvements in students' practical engineering abilities, system-level design skills, and complex problem-solving capabilities compared with traditional teaching approaches. These results suggest that the proposed model offers a scalable and effective paradigm for reforming foundational engineering courses for non-electrical majors in the context of Emerging Engineering Education.  © 2025 IEEE.
