AB  - While most predictive models in education focus on accuracy or dropout, they often overlook the learner’s internal belief in their capacity to succeed—self-efficacy. This study operationalizes self-efficacy not through surveys, but as a dynamic behavioral trajectory extracted from the massive EdNet dataset. By aggregating learner activity into 24-h windows, we engineered features representing process regulation, error recovery, and adaptive persistence to construct a novel composite metric: the SE_index. Using Random Forest regressors and cluster analysis, we disentangled the predictors of three distinct outcomes: final achievement, learning improvement, and discontinuity. The results reveal a critical dissociation: while final achievement is primarily anchored by practice intensity (problem-solving volume) and pacing-related time allocation, learning improvement is distinctly driven by regulatory signals, such as self-correction after errors and response-time variability. Furthermore, we identified that streak breakdowns are better predicted by routine instability (e.g., irregularities in study times) than by low performance. To validate these patterns, we applied K-means and time-series-based clustering to 30-day trajectories, revealing distinct learner prototypes that differentiate ‘short-lived trial users’ from ‘sustained, high-intensity learners’ based on their SE_index scores. These findings suggest that AIED systems must move beyond accuracy-centric metrics, incorporating rhythm and process-regulation signals to enable self-efficacy-aware monitoring and intervention. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.
