TI  - Student Acceptance of Large Language Models for Algorithm Learning: A Technology Acceptance Model Approach
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 5th International Conference on Intelligent Cybernetics Technology and Applications, ICICyTA 2025
SP  - 828
EP  - 833
DO  - 10.1109/ICICYTA68677.2025.11362389
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033661454?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Computing, Telkom University, Bandung, Indonesia
AB  - Learning algorithms and programming creates considerable challenges for students due to the abstract and technical characteristics of computational concepts. In contrast, the rapid adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT offers potential benefits for enhancing learning; yet, tangible proof concerning student acceptance in technical education areas remains limited. This research investigates students’ perceptions and acceptance of LLMs as educational tools in algorithm education, employing the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) framework through a quantitative methodology involving 108 respondents from informatics and computer engineering major. Data was collected via validated online questionnaires and analyzed using Structural Equation Modeling (SEM), with instruments exhibiting acceptable to good reliability across constructs (Cronbach’s α ranging from 0.623 to 0.845). The results show that Perceived Usefulness (PU) is the most significant predictor of Behavioral Intention (BI) to utilize LLMs (β = 0.552, p < 0.001). In contrast, Perceived Ease of Use (PEOU) demonstrates considerable indirect effects via PU (path coefficient = 0.712, p < 0.001), rather than a direct impact on BI. AI Literacy surfaced as a notable secondary predictor (β = 0.276, p < 0.001), while Ethical Concern exhibited a negligible impact (β = -0.027, p > 0.05). The integrated model accounts for 55.1% of the variance in BI and 50.7% in PU, thus validating the relevance and strength of TAM in technical education context. the importance of demonstrating tangible benefits and developing student AI competencies to enhance effective LLM deployment, providing empirical evidence for creating evidence-driven educational practices and institutional policies for the integration of AI tools in algorithmic education. © 2025 IEEE.
